* commit 408f1b4f50cf1f1aa54a6b3120bf5fe12654d902
| Author: Vladimir Reshetnikov <v.reshetnikov@gmail.com>
| Date:   Tue May 5 15:16:30 2026 -0700
| 
|     docs: rephrase reification motivation; highlight [list] and [max]
|     
|     Two unrelated improvements:
|     
|     1. The §2.5.4 motivation for reifying [ack] used the magnitude
|        estimate "tens of thousands of nodes" for the would-be unrolled
|        numeral [tS (tS (... tO))] of [ack 5 42 10002].  This is
|        spectacularly wrong in the *understatement* direction: the unrolled
|        tower has ack(5,42,10002) successor nodes, which is pentational,
|        far above tetration, far above any number expressible in any
|        physical unit.  Replaced the specific estimate with a qualitative
|        description -- "one node per unit in the value, and ack 5 42 10002
|        is astronomically larger than any quantity that could fit in a
|        finite depth budget -- or, for that matter, in the observable
|        universe" -- and added a closing clause noting the contrast: nested
|        [Nat.recursion]s let a *shallow* tree denote a very large number.
|     
|     2. Added [list] and [max] to [morekeywords].  Both are stdlib
|        names that recur in our listings:
|     
|           list -- in Fixpoint signatures like
|                   [Fixpoint termsUpTo (n : nat) : list term :=]
|                   and the [maxBy] parameter [(l : list T) : T :=].
|     
|           max  -- in [type_depth] and [term_depth] which both call
|                   [max] from [Coq.Init.Nat] (or its [Nat.max] variant)
|                   for the binary maximum.
|     
|        Both now render bold-blue, consistent with [nat], [Nat],
|        [iter], [recursion], [List], etc.  Visual consequence: the
|        recursive [max] nesting in the [tLam] case of [term_depth]
|        ([S (max (max (type_depth A) (type_depth B)) (term_depth body))])
|        reads as a uniform tactic-style chain of operators rather than
|        an alternating mix of black [max]es and blue [type_depth]s.
