* commit 74399d5ecead04939fe3d908bf6a0d7eb15f7c0c
| Author: Vladimir Reshetnikov <v.reshetnikov@gmail.com>
| Date:   Tue May 5 13:20:11 2026 -0700
| 
|     docs: fix [<->] rendering, add [In] to listings keyword list
|     
|     Two fixes:
|     
|     1. [<->] was rendering as [←>] -- arrow followed by literal [>] --
|        instead of the intended bidirectional arrow [↔].  An empirical
|        minimal test confirmed that [listings] tries literate patterns in
|        *reverse declaration order* (last-declared, first-tried), opposite
|        to what one would naively assume:
|     
|           literate=
|             {<->}{...}3   {<-}{...}2  =>  matches [<-] first  =>  [←>]
|             {<-}{...}2    {<->}{...}3  =>  matches [<->] first =>  [↔]
|     
|        Reordered the literate spec so [<->] now appears AFTER [<-] and
|        [->].  All three of [<-], [->], [<->] now render correctly:
|     
|           <-  -> ←
|           ->  -> →
|           <-> -> ↔
|     
|        The same rule of thumb -- "longer overlapping pattern declared
|        later" -- applies to any future overlapping literate rules.
|     
|     2. Added [In] to [morekeywords].  [In] is [List.In], the standard
|        library's "element membership" predicate ([forall A a l, In a l :
|        Prop]).  It appears repeatedly in our [maxBy_In] /
|        [lowerbound_maxBy] / [natsUpTo_correct] / [typesUpTo_correct] /
|        [termsUpTo_correct] lemma statements, and is now keyword-blue
|        for visual consistency with other stdlib names already in the
|        list ([Forall2], [nth_error], [Some], [None], [nil], [cons],
|        [iter], [recursion], [Nat]).
